{-
   The Linux system-call layer, for x86-64.

   **`.x64.yana`, and that is the point of the name** - Analysis-Modules.md §2.5 and §4.2.1. The call
   numbers below are the x86-64 table and nothing else: on arm64 `read` is 63 rather than 0, and
   every other number here differs too. Until a file could say which machine it was for, this said
   it in a comment and was compiled for every one of them - including JS, where a syscall is not a
   thing that exists. An architecture selector excludes a JS build for the same reason it excludes
   arm64, so the one word replaces both facts.

   An arm64 port is `Linux.arm64.yana` beside this, and nothing else has to change to accept it.
-}

-- amd64 Linux call numbers.
let sysRead = 0 :: Size
let sysWrite = 1 :: Size
let sysOpen = 2 :: Size
let sysClose = 3 :: Size
let sysStat = 4 :: Size
let sysFileStat = 5 :: Size
let sysLinkStat = 6 :: Size
let sysSeek = 8 :: Size
let sysMmap = 9 :: Size
let sysMunmap = 11 :: Size
let sysReadAt = 17 :: Size
let sysWriteAt = 18 :: Size
let sysExit = 60 :: Size
let sysProcessId = 39 :: Size
let sysSync = 74 :: Size
let sysTruncate = 77 :: Size
let sysRename = 82 :: Size
let sysMakeDirectory = 83 :: Size
let sysRemoveDirectory = 84 :: Size
let sysUnlink = 87 :: Size
let sysDirectoryEntries = 217 :: Size

-- PROT_READ | PROT_WRITE, and MAP_PRIVATE | MAP_ANONYMOUS.
let protReadWrite = 3 :: Size
let mapPrivateAnonymous = 34 :: Size

-- Maps `size` bytes of zeroed, readable and writable address space, or null if the kernel
-- refused. mmap reports failure as a small negative value rather than as an error flag, which is
-- why the result is checked as a number before it becomes a pointer.
pub fn mapMemory(size: Size) -> %U8:
    let result = syscall6(sysMmap, 0, size, protReadWrite, mapPrivateAnonymous, -1, 0)
    if result < 0 then return null()

    return bitcast(result) :: %U8

pub fn unmapMemory(from: %U8, size: Size) -> Size = syscall2(sysMunmap, bitcast(from) :: Size, size)

{-
   The file calls, and what they all have in common - Analysis-Library.md §2.9.

   **Every one of them answers the kernel's own number**, which is a count or a descriptor when it
   worked and the negated errno when it did not. Nothing here turns that into a `Result`, and that
   is the line this file has always drawn: it knows one kernel's call numbers and flag values and
   nothing about what a program wants them to mean. `File` is where a negative answer becomes a
   `FileError`, and it is the only reader of the `err*` numbers below.

   **A path is an address here rather than a `String`**, for the same reason. What the kernel takes
   is a NUL-terminated byte sequence, which is not what a `String` is - a run and a live prefix, with
   nothing after it - so somebody has to make the terminated copy, and that somebody has to be the
   layer that has a string type to copy *into*. See `terminated` in `File`.
-}
{-
   `open`'s flag bits, as a type rather than as nineteen bare integers - Analysis-Language.md §5.2.

   **This is deliberately not a record of `Bool` fields with pinned bit positions**, which is the
   feature the complaint about bare `I64`s reads as asking for and which could not say what `open`
   takes. `readOnly`/`writeOnly`/`readWrite` at 0, 1 and 2 are a two-bit *enumeration* in the low
   bits rather than three independent flags, and `openTemporary` is 4259840, which is
   `__O_TMPFILE | O_DIRECTORY` - a multi-bit combination no bit position names. A bit-position
   feature would have produced a type that quietly cannot hold what the syscall wants.

   A newtype over the integer says everything that was missing and nothing that is false: the
   constants are distinct from every other number in the program, `or` and `and` compose them
   through `Bitwise`, `if flags:` asks `Truth`, and Repr unwraps the whole thing so it costs nothing.

   `Bitwise` and not `Integral`, which is the distinction the two classes exist to draw: `Integral`'s
   superclass chain is `Num` and then `FromInt`, so joining it to get four bitwise names would also
   mean saying what `flags * flags` and the literal `3` are - and there is no answer, which is the
   whole reason this is a newtype rather than an `I64`.

   The two instances are *derived* - Analysis-Derive.md §3's `newtype` shape. What the clause expands
   to is the four unwrap-apply-rewrap bodies and the one truthiness test that used to be written out
   below it, and the reason it can be a clause rather than a compiler rule is that whether a wrapper
   is transparent to a class is the class's decision: `Bitwise` over a newtype is the content's
   `Bitwise` with the name put back on, and that sentence is true of `Bitwise` rather than of
   `OpenFlags`.

   `!flags` is deliberately not a thing this type has. `!` asks `Truth` and answers `Bool`, so it
   means "no bits set" wherever it appears; the complement is `not(flags)`, which is a different
   question with a different name. Those used to be one operator, and `if empty` was False while
   `if !empty` was True.
-}
pub alias qualified OpenFlags = Size deriving (Bitwise, Truth)

-- The low two bits are the access mode and are a small enumeration rather than a set - `readOnly`
-- is zero, so an open that asks for nothing asks for reading - and everything above them is a flag
-- that ors in. `File`'s `FileAccess` and `CreateMode` are what a program writes instead; these are
-- what those become.
pub let openReadOnly = OpenFlags(0)
pub let openWriteOnly = OpenFlags(1)
pub let openReadWrite = OpenFlags(2)
pub let openCreate = OpenFlags(64)
pub let openExclusive = OpenFlags(128)
pub let openTruncate = OpenFlags(512)
pub let openAppend = OpenFlags(1024)
pub let openSync = OpenFlags(4096)
pub let openDirect = OpenFlags(16384)
pub let openDirectory = OpenFlags(65536)
pub let openTemporary = OpenFlags(4259840)

-- The permission bits a created file gets, which the same call takes in its third position and
-- which mean nothing at all unless the open creates something. A second newtype rather than a
-- second use of the first: handing `open` its flags where it wanted its mode is exactly the mistake
-- two bare `I64`s in a row invite.
pub alias qualified FileMode = Size deriving (Bitwise, Truth)

pub fn fileMode(bits: Size) -> FileMode = FileMode(bits)
pub fn modeShifted(mode: FileMode, by: Size) -> FileMode = FileMode(mode.0 `shl` by)

pub fn openPath(path: %U8, flags: OpenFlags, mode: FileMode) -> Size =
    syscall3(sysOpen, bitcast(path) :: Size, flags.0, mode.0)

pub fn closeFile(handle: Size) -> Size = syscall1(sysClose, handle)

pub fn readFile(handle: Size, to: %U8, count: Size) -> Size =
    syscall3(sysRead, handle, bitcast(to) :: Size, count)

pub fn writeFile(handle: Size, from: %U8, count: Size) -> Size =
    syscall3(sysWrite, handle, bitcast(from) :: Size, count)

{-
   A 64-bit file offset, in a syscall argument register.

   `pread64`, `pwrite64`, `ftruncate` and `lseek` all take an `off_t`, which is sixty-four bits on
   thirty-two-bit Linux too - Analysis-Modules.md §4.2.1's file-offset row, which is the counterexample
   that makes the `Size` audit a per-parameter job rather than a rewrite. So the offset stays `I64` in
   every signature here and is narrowed at the one place it becomes a machine word.

   **The narrowing is the identity, because this file is x86-64.** A word is sixty-four bits on this
   architecture and an offset fits one; the compiler cannot know that, because `Size` is deliberately
   a width no stage above `repr` has - so what would otherwise be an unstated assumption is written
   out, once, with its reason. A thirty-two-bit port does not copy this line: that ABI splits an
   offset across *two* argument registers, which is a different call rather than a narrower one, and
   the file that makes it is `Linux.x86.yana` beside this one.
-}
fn offsetWord(at: I64) -> Size = truncate(at) :: Size

-- The positional pair, which is `pread64`/`pwrite64` and not a seek and a read. The file position
-- is untouched by both, which is the whole of what makes them a second pair rather than a
-- convenience over the first.
pub fn readFileAt(handle: Size, to: %U8, count: Size, at: I64) -> Size =
    syscall4(sysReadAt, handle, bitcast(to) :: Size, count, offsetWord(at))

pub fn writeFileAt(handle: Size, from: %U8, count: Size, at: I64) -> Size =
    syscall4(sysWriteAt, handle, bitcast(from) :: Size, count, offsetWord(at))


pub fn syncFile(handle: Size) -> Size = syscall1(sysSync, handle)

pub fn truncateFile(handle: Size, size: I64) -> Size = syscall2(sysTruncate, handle, offsetWord(size))

{-
   `struct stat`, as the record the kernel fills - Analysis-Language.md §5.2.

   What this replaces is six offsets and a `Run(U8)` read with `wordAt`: a C structure layout
   described as arithmetic, in the one place in the library where the layout is an ABI fact rather
   than a call number. `@layout(c)` is what makes the record *be* that layout - declaration order,
   one group, C's own padding - so the sizes and the offsets are the compiler's answer rather than
   six constants somebody has to keep in step with a kernel header.

   A hundred and forty-four bytes on amd64, which is exactly the `statBytes` the arithmetic used.
   Every field defaults to zero, so `FileStatus {}` is the buffer to hand the kernel; the three
   reserved words at the end are the kernel's own `__unused`, named rather than left as a hole
   because a field somebody can see is a field nobody re-derives.

   The three times are a pair of words each - seconds and nanoseconds - and the mode is a `U32`
   holding the type bits above the permission ones.
-}
@layout(c)
pub data FileStatus {
    device: U64 = 0,
    inode: U64 = 0,
    links: U64 = 0,
    mode: U32 = 0,
    userId: U32 = 0,
    groupId: U32 = 0,
    padding: U32 = 0,
    representedDevice: U64 = 0,
    size: I64 = 0,
    blockSize: I64 = 0,
    blocks: I64 = 0,
    accessedSeconds: I64 = 0,
    accessedNanos: I64 = 0,
    modifiedSeconds: I64 = 0,
    modifiedNanos: I64 = 0,
    changedSeconds: I64 = 0,
    changedNanos: I64 = 0,
    reserved0: I64 = 0,
    reserved1: I64 = 0,
    reserved2: I64 = 0
}

{-
   The three `stat` calls, into a record the caller supplies.

   Three and not one, because which of them a program wants is a question about symbolic links and
   the answer differs per call site: `statPath` follows them, `statLink` reports the link itself, and
   `statFile` asks about an open descriptor and so has no link to have an opinion about. The C++ this
   is ported from has only the first and the third, which is why its `Info::isLink` could never be
   true - see `linkInfo` in `File`.

   `&into` rather than an address, which is what moves the last piece of layout arithmetic out of
   `File`: the caller writes `let &status = FileStatus {}` and reads named fields off it afterwards,
   and the one place that still knows the buffer is bytes is the line below that takes its address.
-}
fn statusAddress(&into: FileStatus) -> Size = bitcast(addressOf(into)) :: Size

pub fn statPath(path: %U8, &into: FileStatus) -> Size =
    syscall2(sysStat, bitcast(path) :: Size, statusAddress(into))

pub fn statLink(path: %U8, &into: FileStatus) -> Size =
    syscall2(sysLinkStat, bitcast(path) :: Size, statusAddress(into))

pub fn statFile(handle: Size, &into: FileStatus) -> Size =
    syscall2(sysFileStat, handle, statusAddress(into))

{-
   What the mode's type bits say a directory entry is - Analysis-Language.md §5.1.

   `S_IFMT` is the mask and these are the three kinds anything here asks about. A pinned value each,
   because these are the kernel's numbers and not a declaration order that happens to agree with
   them, and `fromValue` is how the masked mode becomes one - a mode naming a socket or a device is
   a kind this set does not have, which is what `Maybe` says.
-}
pub data FileKind = @value(16384) DirectoryKind | @value(32768) RegularKind | @value(40960) LinkKind

pub let modeTypeMask = FileMode(61440)

-- Through `I32` rather than straight to `Size`, which is the idiom `slotFor` in `Core/Map.yana`
-- writes and for the same reason: a `U32` does not widen into a signed word, because on a target
-- whose word is thirty-two bits its top half would not fit. Reinterpreting first says that these
-- are mode bits and never a large unsigned number.
pub fn kindOf(mode: U32) -> Maybe(FileKind) =
    fromValue((FileMode((bitcast(mode) :: I32) :: Size) `and` modeTypeMask).0)

pub fn removePath(path: %U8) -> Size = syscall1(sysUnlink, bitcast(path) :: Size)

pub fn renamePath(from: %U8, to: %U8) -> Size =
    syscall2(sysRename, bitcast(from) :: Size, bitcast(to) :: Size)

pub fn makeDirectory(path: %U8, mode: FileMode) -> Size =
    syscall2(sysMakeDirectory, bitcast(path) :: Size, mode.0)

pub fn removeDirectoryPath(path: %U8) -> Size = syscall1(sysRemoveDirectory, bitcast(path) :: Size)

-- `getdents64`, which is the only reading call here that answers a *block* of variable-length
-- records rather than bytes: zero means the directory ended, and a positive answer is how many
-- bytes of `into` the kernel filled. The record layout is `DirectoryRecord` below.
pub fn readDirectoryEntries(handle: Size, into: %U8, count: Size) -> Size =
    syscall3(sysDirectoryEntries, handle, bitcast(into) :: Size, count)

{-
   `dirent64`'s fixed head, by the same rule `FileStatus` is a record: the inode number, the offset
   of the next record, this record's length, and one byte of type.

   The NUL-terminated name follows immediately and is *not* a field, because its length is not one:
   `length` covers the padding after the name, so the name is read by scanning from the end of this
   head. That one piece of arithmetic is what a variable-length record costs and it is all that is
   left of the six offsets this replaced.
-}
@layout(c)
pub data DirectoryRecord {inode: U64, nextOffset: I64, length: U16, kind: U8}

-- Where the name starts, in bytes from the start of the record. One past the head, which is what
-- `dirent64` says and what `@layout(c)` makes the compiler agree with.
pub let entryNameOffset = 19 :: Size

-- `DT_DIR` and the two other kinds `getdents64` reports that anything here asks about. `DT_UNKNOWN`
-- is 0 and is what a file system that does not fill the byte answers, which is why this is partial.
pub data EntryKind = @value(4) EntryDirectory | @value(8) EntryRegular | @value(10) EntryLink

{-
   `lseek`'s three bases, as the enumeration they are - Analysis-Language.md §5.1.

   `data Whence = FromStart | FromCurrent | FromEnd` already lowered to 0, 1 and 2 in declaration
   order; what it could not do was *say* so, and the cost of that showed at exactly this boundary.
   Turning one into the number the syscall takes was two compares and two selects computing the
   identity function, because the mapping lived in a `match` nothing had reason to recognise.
   `@value` pins it, `valueOf` is a cast, and `seekFile` can no longer be handed the wrong integer.
-}
pub data Whence = @value(0) FromStart | @value(1) FromCurrent | @value(2) FromEnd

pub fn seekFile(handle: Size, offset: I64, whence: Whence) -> I64 =
    syscall3(sysSeek, handle, offsetWord(offset), truncate(valueOf(whence)) :: Size) :: I64

{-
   The errno numbers `File` maps, as one type - Analysis-Language.md §5.1.

   They are one kernel's, exactly as the call numbers above are: `ENOENT` is 2 on Linux and 2 on
   every other Unix by accident of history rather than by agreement, and the numbers that matter
   most here - `EDQUOT` at 122, `ENAMETOOLONG` at 36 - differ between them. So the whole of what a
   second platform has to restate lives in this file, and `File` names these rather than integers.

   Nineteen `let`s became one type, and what that bought is not brevity: a `File` that maps errno to
   its own closed set used to do it with nineteen `if code == err...` comparisons, because a bare
   name in a pattern *binds* and `match code: errNotFound -> ...` would have matched everything.
   With a type there is a `match` to write, and the compiler checks it covers what it says it does.

   The set is open in practice - the kernel has two hundred numbers and this names nineteen - which
   is what makes `fromValue` the reading direction and `Maybe` its answer.
-}
pub data Errno
  = @value(1)   NotPermitted
  | @value(2)   NoEntry
  | @value(9)   BadHandle
  | @value(12)  OutOfMemory
  | @value(13)  PermissionDenied
  | @value(14)  BadAddress
  | @value(16)  DeviceBusy
  | @value(17)  AlreadyExists
  | @value(20)  NotADirectory
  | @value(21)  IsADirectory
  | @value(22)  InvalidArgument
  | @value(23)  TableFull
  | @value(24)  TooManyOpen
  | @value(27)  FileTooLarge
  | @value(28)  NoSpace
  | @value(30)  ReadOnlyFileSystem
  | @value(36)  NameTooLong
  | @value(39)  NotEmpty
  | @value(122) QuotaExceeded

-- The kernel's own number as one of these, or nothing where it is a case this set does not name.
pub fn errnoOf(code: I64) -> Maybe(Errno) = fromValue(code)

-- This process's own identifier, which cannot fail and so is the one call here that answers no
-- error. What wants it is a program that has to name something no other process will name at the
-- same time - a lock file, a scratch directory - and `test/lib/File.yana` is the first.
pub fn processId() -> Size = syscall0(sysProcessId)

pub fn exitProcess(status: Size) -> {}:
    syscall1(sysExit, status)
    return
